Are you sure that the script isn't executing? It should work, but you shouldn't get valid JSON output that jQuery can parse. Don't use print() but cat(). And before you cat() out the repsonse, put relevant parts to list in format like:

list(success = TRUE, output = <something>)

so that it can be serialised properly. The way you're doing it is not returning JSON, but bunch of text. And you may defer loading of text data to JS instead of R (the paste()/print() stuff can and should be handled with JS). Speaking of JS, I'd recommend slightly more hackish .ajax() function, but .post() should work just fine, just skim down the documentation <http://api.jquery.com/jQuery.ajax/> for more options (.post() options are practically the same as in .ajax()). You may want to set dataType: "json" and return JSON from your R script. Right now you aren't generating any JSON (see documentation for toJSON()).

I have pretty outdated webapp here <https://github.com/aL3xa/univar-plot>, with some deprecated RApache directives, but if you read the source, you'll see how it's handled. I also strongly recommend Jeroen Ooms' video <http://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_detailpage&v=7x0UdUghANI>.

aL3xa

